---
layout: base.njk
---
{#
  layouts/cluster.njk — one DPRK actor cluster
  ============================================

  Used as the 11ty `layout` for src/clusters/cluster-page.njk, which paginates
  over the global data array `clusters` (src/_data/clusters.js) and binds each
  entry to `cluster`. Front matter chains this layout to base.njk, and 11ty
  resolves {% block content %} across a `layout:` chain — so, as in
  layouts/hunt.njk and layouts/profile.njk, the block has to be declared here
  explicitly rather than left as a bare HTML body.

  Everything on this page is either written into src/_data/taxonomy.json (the
  prose, the aliases, the further-reading list) or joined onto it by
  clusters.js from material the site already holds (profiles, personas,
  indicators, hunts, citations). Nothing is hand-copied per cluster: adding a
  tag to taxonomy.json produces a page, and tagging a profile or a hunt puts it
  on the right one.

  The three prose fields are arrays of paragraphs rendered with `| safe`, so
  they can carry links into the rest of the site. They are repo-controlled
  editorial copy in a checked-in data file, not input from anywhere else.

  Two client-side scripts are reused here rather than a third written, and each
  is used where it fits. ioc-search.js downloads the compact index and is given
  a `data-ioc-scope` attribute naming this cluster's activity labels, because a
  cluster can hold thousands of indicators and the page cannot. table-filter.js
  filters the citations table, which is already rendered in full as real rows.
  Both hide their own control in the markup and reveal it only when they can
  work, so a reader without JavaScript gets the whole page rather than a dead
  widget. The indicator panel is additionally deferred: it shows its input at
  once and fetches the index on the first keystroke, because this page is read
  for its prose and the index is measured in megabytes.
#}
{% block content %}
<article>

  {% set backLink = { url: "/#actor-cluster-taxonomy", text: "All actor clusters" } %}{% include "partials/back-link.njk" %}

  <header>
    <h1>{{ cluster.label }}</h1>
    {% if cluster.also_known_as and cluster.also_known_as.length %}
    <p class="chip-row">
      <span class="cluster-page__label">Also tracked as</span>
      {% for alias in cluster.also_known_as %}<span class="tag-chip">{{ alias }}</span>{% endfor %}
    </p>
    {% endif %}
    {% if cluster.attack_group %}
    <p class="chip-row">
      <span class="cluster-page__label">MITRE ATT&amp;CK{% if cluster.attack_group.type == "software" %} software{% endif %}</span>
      <a class="tag-chip" href="{{ cluster.attack_group.url }}" rel="noopener noreferrer">{{ cluster.attack_group.id }}</a>
    </p>
    {% endif %}
  </header>

  <section>
    {% for paragraph in cluster.overview | paragraphs %}
    {{ paragraph | wrapParagraph | safe }}
    {% endfor %}

    {# Counts, not claims: each is derived by clusters.js from the tagged
       content itself, so a figure here can never disagree with the section it
       links to. A zero is stated rather than hidden — that this site holds no
       indicators under a cluster is itself worth knowing. #}
    <dl class="hunt-facts">
      <dt>Named individuals</dt>
      <dd>{% if cluster.profiles.length %}<a href="#named-individuals">{{ cluster.profiles.length }}</a> profiled from law-enforcement records{% else %}None profiled on this site yet{% endif %}</dd>
      <dt>Fabricated personas</dt>
      <dd>{% if cluster.personas.length %}<a href="#personas">{{ cluster.personas.length }}</a> reconstructed from reported accounts{% else %}None reconstructed under this tag{% endif %}</dd>
      <dt>Indicators</dt>
      <dd>{% if cluster.indicators.total %}<a href="#indicators">{{ cluster.indicators.total | thousands }}</a> in the searchable index{% else %}None in the searchable index{% endif %}</dd>
      <dt>Hunts</dt>
      <dd>{% if cluster.hunts.length %}<a href="#hunts">{{ cluster.hunts.length }}</a> in the hunt library{% else %}None in the hunt library{% endif %}</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="tradecraft">How it differs from the other clusters here</h2>
    {% for paragraph in cluster.tradecraft | paragraphs %}
    {{ paragraph | wrapParagraph | safe }}
    {% endfor %}
    <p class="chip-row">
      <span class="cluster-page__label">Compare</span>
      {% for other in clusters %}{% if other.id != cluster.id %}<a class="tag-chip" href="{{ other.url }}">{{ other.label }}</a>{% endif %}{% endfor %}
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="observables">What a defender would see</h2>
    {% for paragraph in cluster.defender_view | paragraphs %}
    {{ paragraph | wrapParagraph | safe }}
    {% endfor %}
  </section>

  {% if cluster.profiles.length %}
  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2 id="named-individuals">Named individuals</h2>
      {% if cluster.profiles.length > limits.list %}<p class="ioc-download"><a class="btn btn--secondary" href="/profiles/">All {{ cluster.profiles.length }} profiles</a></p>{% endif %}
    </div>
    <p>
      Every person below is named in a public charging document, sanctions
      record or law-enforcement wanted notice that carries this cluster tag.
      Each profile lists the records it draws on and nothing beyond them.
    </p>
    <ul class="profile-grid">
      {% for profile in cluster.profiles | limit(limits.list) %}{% set tileProfile = profile %}{% include "partials/profile-tile.njk" %}{% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>
  {% endif %}

  {% if cluster.personas.length %}
  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2 id="personas">Fabricated personas</h2>
      <p class="ioc-download"><a class="btn btn--secondary" href="/remote-workers/personas/">All {{ cluster.personas.length }} personas</a></p>
    </div>
    <p>
      Identities assembled from the accounts, addresses and portfolio sites
      reported under one name. A persona is a reporting artefact rather than a
      person: several may be run by one operator, and one may be run by
      several.
    </p>
    <ul class="profile-grid">
      {% for persona in cluster.personas_preview %}{% set cardPersona = persona %}{% include "partials/persona-card.njk" %}{% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>
  {% endif %}

  {% if cluster.hunts.length %}
  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2 id="hunts">Hunts for this cluster</h2>
      <p class="ioc-download"><a class="btn btn--secondary" href="/hunts/">Full hunt library</a></p>
    </div>
    <ul class="hunt-grid">
      {% for entry in cluster.hunts | limit(limits.list) %}{% set cardHunt = entry %}{% include "partials/hunt-card.njk" %}{% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>
  {% endif %}

  <section>
    <h2 id="indicators">Indicators</h2>
    {% if cluster.indicators.total %}
    <p>
      The {{ cluster.indicators.total | thousands }} indicators the index holds
      under this cluster. The complete index is on
      <a href="/remote-workers/">Remote Workers</a>.
    </p>

    {# Scoped by data-ioc-scope, read by src/assets/js/ioc-search.js. The type
       chips below are counted over this cluster alone, so the figure on a chip
       matches what pressing it returns. Hidden until the index has loaded. #}
    {# data-ioc-defer: a cluster page is read for its prose, and the index is
       3.6MB. The fifty indicators rendered below at build time are what most
       readers want; the download waits until somebody actually searches. #}
    <div class="ioc-search" hidden data-ioc-search data-ioc-defer data-ioc-scope="{{ cluster.indicators.scope }}">
      {% set searchBox = { id: "ioc-search-input", label: "Search " + cluster.label + " indicators", placeholder: "Email, GitHub or LinkedIn account, persona, name, or any word from a report", count: "data-ioc-count" } %}{% include "partials/search-box.njk" %}
      {% set filterChips = { dimension: "type", short: "Type", label: "Filter by indicator type", allLabel: "All types (" + (cluster.indicators.total | thousands) + ")", counts: cluster.indicators.counts_by_type, formatValue: "replace-underscore" } %}
      {% include "partials/filter-chips.njk" %}
      {% include "partials/ioc-download-controls.njk" %}
    </div>

    {% set iocTable = { results: true } %}{% include "partials/ioc-table.njk" %}

    {% include "partials/show-more.njk" %}

    <noscript>
      <p class="form-hint">
        Searching requires JavaScript. The
        {{ cluster.indicators.recent | length }} indicators for this cluster
        with the most recent source dates are listed below, and the complete index is available
        as <a href="/assets/data/ioc-index.csv">CSV</a> or
        <a href="/assets/data/ioc-index.json">JSON</a>.
      </p>
    </noscript>

    {% set iocTable = { records: cluster.indicators.recent } %}{% include "partials/ioc-table.njk" %}
    {% else %}
    <p>
      The index holds no indicators under this cluster, whose reporting is
      mostly narrative; start with the further reading below.
    </p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="evidence">Where the evidence comes from</h2>
    {% if cluster.sources.length %}
    {% set tableFilter = { target: "#cluster-sources-table", noun: "records", id: "cluster-source-search", label: "Search these records", placeholder: "Publisher, title, year, or the profile or hunt it supports", page: limits.list } %}
    {% include "partials/table-filter.njk" %}

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="results-table" id="cluster-sources-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Record</th>
            <th scope="col">Publisher</th>
            <th scope="col">Published</th>
            <th scope="col">Supports</th>
            <th scope="col">Indicators</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {% for source in cluster.sources %}
          <tr>
            <td>
              {% set externalLink = { url: source.url, text: source.title } %}{% include "partials/external-link.njk" %}
              {% if source.items.length %}
              <span class="ioc-result__meta">{{ source.items | join(", ") | truncate(160) }}</span>
              {% endif %}
            </td>
            <td>{{ source.publisher | default("—", true) }}</td>
            <td class="cell-date">{{ source.published | default("—", true) }}</td>
            <td>{% for area in source.areas %}<span class="tag-chip">{{ area }}</span>{% endfor %}</td>
            <td>{{ source.indicator_count | thousands }}</td>
          </tr>
          {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    {% include "partials/table-filter-more.njk" %}
    {% else %}
    <p>
      Nothing on this site cites a record under this cluster tag. Every source
      the site cites is on <a href="/sources/">Sources</a>.
    </p>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {% if cluster.further_reading and cluster.further_reading.length %}
  <section>
    <h2 id="further-reading">Further reading</h2>
    <p>Published research on this cluster. None of it is this site&rsquo;s work.</p>
    <ul class="reading-list">
      {% for item in cluster.further_reading %}
      <li>
        {% set externalLink = { url: item.url, text: item.title } %}{% include "partials/external-link.njk" %}
        <span class="reading-list__meta">{{ item.publisher }}{% if item.date %} &middot; {{ item.date }}{% endif %}</span>
        <p class="reading-list__note">{{ item.note }}</p>
      </li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>
  {% endif %}

  <section>
    {% set disclaimer = {
      title: "Disclaimer",
      body: 'Cluster names are labels applied by researchers and agencies, not units North Korea publishes. Vendors draw boundaries differently, so a tag records how the cited sources characterise the activity, not a finding of this site. See the <a href="/sources/#sourcing-methodology">sourcing methodology</a>.'
    } %}{% include "partials/disclaimer.njk" %}
  </section>

</article>
{% endblock %}
